 "title": "Git doesn't store diffs",
 "sources": [
  "Pro Git book, 'What is Git?' (https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Getting-Started-What-is-Git%3F, fetched 2026-11-24): most other systems store 'a list of file-based changes'; 'Git doesn't think of or store its data this way'; every commit 'takes a picture of what all your files look like at that moment'; 'if files have not changed, Git doesn't store the file again, just a link to the previous identical file'; SHA-1 hash, 'a 40-character string composed of hexadecimal characters... calculated based on the contents'; 'Git stores everything in its database not by file name but by the hash value of its contents'; 'it's impossible to change the contents of any file or directory without Git knowing about it'.",
  "Simplification: Git can still compress objects into packfiles internally; the video describes the conceptual model from the book."
 ],
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "Most developers think Git saves the changes you make. So, diffs or full copies? Pick one.",
   "caps": "GIT SAVES *CHANGES? | *DIFFS | OR *COPIES?"
  },
  {
   "text": "Neither, exactly. Every commit is a snapshot of your whole project.",
   "caps": "*NEITHER | A *SNAPSHOT | WHOLE *PROJECT"
  },
  {
   "text": "But unchanged files aren't stored again. Git just links to the copy it already has.",
   "caps": "*UNCHANGED FILES | NOT *STORED AGAIN | JUST A *LINK"
  },
  {
   "text": "How does it know a file is the same? Every file is named by a hash of its contents.",
   "caps": "SAME FILE? | NAMED BY *HASH | OF ITS *CONTENTS"
  },
  {
   "text": "That's the forty character SHA-1 you see in every commit ID.",
   "caps": "*40 CHARACTERS | *SHA-1 | COMMIT *ID"
  },
  {
   "text": "Same content means the same hash, so identical files are stored only once.",
   "caps": "SAME *CONTENT | SAME *HASH | STORED *ONCE"
  },
  {
   "text": "And change one byte, and the hash changes, so history can't be edited without Git knowing.",
   "caps": "ONE *BYTE | NEW *HASH | GIT *KNOWS"
  },
  {
   "text": "So Git is a database of snapshots, keyed by hash. Did you pick diffs?",
   "caps": "*SNAPSHOTS | KEYED BY *HASH | DID YOU PICK *DIFFS?"
  }
 ],
